mean?

01:44:21.580 --> 01:44:25.180
Well, it's the difference in the way the
calculations are done.

01:44:25.180 --> 01:44:33.740
So, we would zoom in at the differences
and we would look to see if the company is
carrying a large amount of inventory.

01:44:34.460 --> 01:44:41.660
And what is the inventory and can the
inventory even be sold and converted into
cash?

01:44:43.340 --> 01:44:51.900
So, we would ask these questions and
figure out which is a more accurate
picture of the liquidity

01:44:51.900 --> 01:44:58.140
and the financial health of the company,
whether it's the current ratio or the
quick ratio.

01:44:59.500 --> 01:45:04.780
So, if we have a low quick ratio, but a
high current ratio,

01:45:05.820 --> 01:45:10.860
then the inventory that the company is
carrying on its balance sheet,

01:45:10.860 --> 01:45:14.940
if it can't be sold and quickly converted
into cash,

01:45:14.940 --> 01:45:21.340
then the high current ratio may be
flattering the position of the company on
paper

01:45:21.340 --> 01:45:26.300
and the reality is, is that the company is
at or near insolvency.

01:45:27.820 --> 01:45:33.260
Next up is our working capital to total
assets ratio.

01:45:33.820 --> 01:45:37.580
Again, taking the data from the past
balance sheet.

01:45:38.300 --> 01:45:42.620
It is calculated as working capital over
total assets,

01:45:43.180 --> 01:45:47.980
where working capital is current assets
minus current liabilities.

01:45:47.980 --> 01:45:54.140
So, we take current assets, we take away
current liabilities.

01:45:54.140 --> 01:45:59.740
So, current assets minus current
liabilities, and then we divide by total
assets.

01:45:59.740 --> 01:46:08.220
So, we have a useful guide here from an
accounting perspective that would
constitute a good,

01:46:08.220 --> 01:46:16.300
satisfactory, unsatisfactory, or critical
working capital to total assets ratio.

01:46:16.300 --> 01:46:29.100
So, for example, if we had a company that
had current assets of 2.345 billion and
current liabilities of 2.166 billion,

01:46:29.660 --> 01:46:36.140
we would minus off the liabilities from
the assets, and then we would divide by
total assets.

01:46:36.140 --> 01:46:48.540
So, in this example, 6.498 billion, we
would get to a figure of 0.0275, which is
2.75%.

01:46:48.540 --> 01:46:55.980
From an accounting perspective, this would
be seen as a critical position for a
company.

01:46:56.620 --> 01:47:06.460
So, according to the table, if you do the
calculation for a company for working
capital to total assets ratio,

01:47:06.460 --> 01:47:16.140
and you get a number of 0.3 or 30% or
above, this would be considered, from an
accounting perspective,

01:47:16.140 --> 01:47:30.140
a very good, very strong position for a
company between 0.15 and 0.3, so 15% and
30%, this would constitute a satisfactory
position.

01:47:31.100 --> 01:47:40.300
Between 0 and 15, this would constitute,
from an accounting perspective, an
unsatisfactory position,

01:47:40.300 --> 01:47:47.020
and a ratio that's negative, so below 0,
it would be critical.

01:47:47.020 --> 01:47:53.020
But, if it's very, very low and close to
0, it is essentially critical.

01:47:53.020 --> 01:47:59.820
It's hovering just above 0, and it's
essentially in a very bad position.

01:47:59.820 --> 01:48:05.420
The company is in a bad position. It is
basically in a critical scenario.

01:48:06.780 --> 01:48:14.620
So, in the context of when we're assessing
something, a company, whether it's going
to be a potential

01:48:14.620 --> 01:48:21.020
turnaround or potential value trap
scenario, this is an important measure.

01:48:21.660 --> 01:48:28.620
So, in this situation where we have a
critical condition, management need to
make long-term asset

01:48:28.620 --> 01:48:35.500
disposals and perhaps even raise capital
via an equity or debt offering.

01:48:35.500 --> 01:48:38.380
So, they have to restructure the business.

01:48:38.380 --> 01:48:43.420
And on top of that, they will also have to
turn around the operation as well.

01:48:44.780 --> 01:48:50.540
If they can make some asset disposals,
even if there are non-favorable prices,

01:48:51.180 --> 01:48:55.740
then we could see a turnaround in the cash
position.

01:48:55.740 --> 01:48:58.540
They can use that to pay down some debt.

01:48:58.540 --> 01:49:07.340
And we can therefore improve the numerator
in the working capital to total assets
ratio and therefore

01:49:07.340 --> 01:49:14.220
improve the working capital position and
the working capital asset ratio itself.

01:49:14.220 --> 01:49:20.300
Now, what if the numerator of working
capital was actually negative?

01:49:20.300 --> 01:49:25.420
So, our current assets minus current
liabilities comes out as negative.

01:49:25.420 --> 01:49:29.100
Then, of course, we're going to be in a
very critical position.

01:49:29.100 --> 01:49:32.460
The ratio would just be negative.

01:49:33.980 --> 01:49:40.460
Next up on the balance sheet, we can look
at book value per share or BVPS.

01:49:41.260 --> 01:49:50.940
So, book value per share is stockholders'
equity minus preferred stock divided by
average shares outstanding.

01:49:52.380 --> 01:50:00.940
So, book value per share is basically the
ratio of equity that's available to common
stockholders

01:50:01.500 --> 01:50:04.940
against the number of shares outstanding.

01:50:05.820 --> 01:50:17.420
And when we compare this to the current
stock price in the market, the book value
per share can provide us with information
on how the company is valued.

01:50:17.420 --> 01:50:24.220
So, as in a premium or discount to book
value per share and the size of that
difference.

01:50:25.660 --> 01:50:33.180
Now, in the context of assessing whether a
stock is a potential turnaround or even a
potential value trap,

01:50:33.820 --> 01:50:39.820
and in combination with other quantitative
and qualitative metrics,

01:50:40.540 --> 01:50:47.900
it gives us a good idea on the opportunity
and the risk of a potential trade idea.

01:50:47.900 --> 01:50:51.980
So, the upside and downside on a long or
short position.

01:50:53.020 --> 01:50:59.420
As a side note, by the way, for our
calculation, we are using the average
number of shares outstanding

01:51:00.060 --> 01:51:08.380
options, because the closing period amount
may be different if there was a stock
issuance or

01:51:08.380 --> 01:51:10.700
a share buyback program in the market.

01:51:12.060 --> 01:51:16.940
Now, we can run through an example here of
book value per share.

01:51:16.940 --> 01:51:24.860
So, let's say we have a company, XYZ
Corporation, and they have $4 billion of
stockholders' equity,

01:51:25.500 --> 01:51:33.980
of which $1 billion is preferred stock,
and they have 60 million shares
outstanding over the period.

01:51:35.100 --> 01:51:45.020
So, our book value per share would be
calculated as $4 billion minus $1 billion,
so the $3 billion divided by 60 million
shares.

01:51:45.580 --> 01:51:49.340
So, that would give us a book value per
share of $2 billion, so that would give us
a book value per share of $50.

01:51:50.300 --> 01:52:01.260
Now, if the current stock price is trading
at $62, we can say that the stock is
trading on 1.24 times book value per
share.

01:52:02.460 --> 01:52:09.500
Now, let's say after assessing all other
quantitative metrics and the management
operating plan,

01:52:10.300 --> 01:52:15.100
it might not be sensible to short this
stock, even if you think it's a value
trap.

01:52:15.900 --> 01:52:20.540
So, even if you've determined that you
think earnings are still going to go
lower.

01:52:21.100 --> 01:52:27.500
So, if earnings and retained earnings
shallow out and management have stopped
the EPS bleed

01:52:28.060 --> 01:52:36.140
before even a new management operating
plan is even fully working, the risk
reward may not even work.

01:52:36.140 --> 01:52:42.700
So, the stock may have traveled
significantly lower and value buyers could
be circling around

01:52:42.700 --> 01:52:52.620
at the level of the book value per share
price, so this would indicate that we're
probably in a phase 4 or phase 5 scenario.

01:52:52.620 --> 01:52:57.100
And this is exactly why we avoid shorting
these situations.

01:52:58.060 --> 01:53:07.340
Because a short position in this scenario
may only provide $12 of upside in terms of
P&L on the short.

01:53:07.340 --> 01:53:12.140
So, $12 upside of profit, but have
infinite downside.

01:53:12.780 --> 01:53:19.260
We want to be seeing much better ROI on
our trades than that for shorts.

01:53:19.260 --> 01:53:23.740
So, this is why we need to be in phase 2
and phase 3.

01:53:24.780 --> 01:53:29.820
We would much rather be short stocks that
are traveling into a situation like this

01:53:30.620 --> 01:53:34.460
rather than shorting stocks that are
actually in this situation.

01:53:35.100 --> 01:53:40.700
So, does this mean the stock is actually
along? Not necessarily.

01:53:40.700 --> 01:53:46.940
If you don't believe in the turnaround
story, so the management operating plan,
and you believe that

01:53:46.940 --> 01:53:55.340
it's a potential value trap, then if the
stock has already traveled,
non-participation is always an option.

01:53:55.340 --> 01:54:05.020
Something else to consider is that a
company can also increase its book value
per share by just buying back stock.

01:54:05.020 --> 01:54:09.500
So, reducing the share count, the amount
of shares outstanding.

01:54:09.500 --> 01:54:17.900
If the company has a decent amount of
short-term assets like cash and marketable
securities,

01:54:17.900 --> 01:54:22.220
they might actually go and surprise the
market with a share buyback program,

01:54:22.780 --> 01:54:27.820
whilst deploying the new management
operating plan for the turnaround.

01:54:27.820 --> 01:54:32.140
And this could cause a stock to really
rally significantly.

01:54:33.420 --> 01:54:38.860
So again, book value per share from our
balance sheet is a metric

01:54:39.660 --> 01:54:42.780
that can't be used simply in isolation.

01:54:43.420 --> 01:54:53.020
We need to look at book value per share in
the context or next to all other balance
sheet ratios

01:54:53.020 --> 01:54:55.500
and financial health metrics.

01:54:57.180 --> 01:55:01.900
Now, we're going to move on from looking
at just a company balance sheet

01:55:02.620 --> 01:55:08.940
to now looking at metrics that combine
data from both a company balance sheet

01:55:08.940 --> 01:55:12.380
and a company's profit and loss statement.

01:55:13.420 --> 01:55:18.300
And the first one we're going to look at
is the sales to assets ratio,

01:55:18.300 --> 01:55:22.540
which is net sales over average total
assets,

01:55:23.100 --> 01:55:29.020
where average total assets is equal to
assets at the start of the year,

01:55:29.020 --> 01:55:33.180
plus assets at the end of the year,
divided by two.

01:55:34.620 --> 01:55:39.900
Now, the sales to assets ratio is
sometimes called the asset turnover ratio.

01:55:40.460 --> 01:55:45.180
And this metric measures the efficiency of
a company.

01:55:45.180 --> 01:55:51.020
How well a company is using its assets to
generate sales.

01:55:51.740 --> 01:55:55.740
And it's almost always calculated on an
annual basis.

01:55:56.300 --> 01:55:58.620
And the higher the ratio, the better.

01:55:59.740 --> 01:56:05.980
However, we do need to be careful when
we're thinking that a sales to assets
ratio

01:56:06.620 --> 01:56:12.380
is useful when we're comparing that number
across industry sectors.

01:56:13.020 --> 01:56:14.540
This is just folly.

01:56:14.540 --> 01:56:18.540
It's foolish because they can differ
wildly.

01:56:18.540 --> 01:56:27.500
So for example, analyzing the sales to
asset ratio of a tech stock versus a
utility that has massive

01:56:27.500 --> 01:56:35.660
fixed asset investment and low sales in
comparison to the investments made is
meaningless.

01:56:36.460 --> 01:56:45.020
You have to analyze sales to assets ratios
within the same sector and through time to
determine

01:56:45.020 --> 01:56:50.700
if asset utilization is either
deteriorating or improving.

01:56:52.380 --> 01:56:58.140
Now, in the context of figuring out if
something is actually a turnaround story
or not,

01:56:58.140 --> 01:57:05.020
what the sales to assets ratio is going to
likely already be low versus the sector.

01:57:05.580 --> 01:57:10.700
But it helps us fill in another gap in
understanding the overall story.

01:57:10.700 --> 01:57:21.260
If a stock has fallen materially already
and we see that the sales to assets ratio
has deteriorated rapidly

01:57:21.820 --> 01:57:28.060
and is now low but stable, then in
combination with other ratios that we've
looked at,

01:57:28.700 --> 01:57:34.860
we may decide that this could be the signs
of the beginning of a turnaround.

01:57:34.860 --> 01:57:40.140
So we are sitting potentially in a phase
four or phase five scenario.

01:57:41.260 --> 01:57:47.020
Ideally, what we'd like to see when we're
sitting in that phase four or phase five
scenario,

01:57:47.020 --> 01:57:54.380
as always, is a management operating plan
that gives some visibility to a turnaround

01:57:54.940 --> 01:57:59.100
and maybe even some noises about
restructuring.

01:58:00.140 --> 01:58:04.220
So there is a flip side to this if we
don't actually see that.

01:58:04.220 --> 01:58:12.620
And we have a low sales to assets ratio
where we have no management operating plan
looking going forward

01:58:13.340 --> 01:58:17.020
and no restructuring plan or activity.

01:58:17.580 --> 01:58:24.380
This may cause us to decide that we should
avoid it as a turnaround long idea

01:58:24.380 --> 01:58:27.100
because it could be potentially a value
trap.

01:58:27.740 --> 01:58:37.500
Now, there is a drawback, a blind spot
that we need to take account of with the
sales to assets ratio calculation

01:58:38.380 --> 01:58:43.660
because it doesn't take into account the
company making asset investments.

01:58:44.300 --> 01:58:52.700
So if the company invests in a new asset
and it's a big investment, the ratio could
look low.

01:58:53.260 --> 01:58:57.020
But future sales estimates could now be
going higher.

01:58:57.580 --> 01:59:02.700
It has to be looked at in the context of a
management operating plan.

01:59:02.700 --> 01:59:06.860
A restructuring plan can really change
everything very quickly.

01:59:08.060 --> 01:59:13.420
Now, another balance sheet profit and loss
statement combo metric that we look at

01:59:13.420 --> 01:59:18.220
is called the return on assets ratio or
the ROA ratio.

01:59:19.180 --> 01:59:21.260
And it can be calculated in two ways.

01:59:21.260 --> 01:59:22.460
There's two ways of doing this.

01:59:23.820 --> 01:59:26.860
ROA can be net income over total assets

01:59:27.500 --> 01:59:35.020
or it can be net income over average total
assets, where again, the average total
assets is assets

01:59:35.020 --> 01:59:39.660
at the start of the year plus assets at
the end of the year divided by two.

01:59:40.700 --> 01:59:49.020
Now, the ROA ratio is similar to the sales
to assets ratio, as in it's an efficiency
metric.

01:59:49.020 --> 01:59:54.380
So it works out how efficient a company is
versus its sector peers.

01:59:54.940 --> 02:00:02.060
However, instead of using the sales number
as the numerator, we use net income.

02:00:02.540 --> 02:00:05.500
So it's a profitability measure.

02:00:05.500 --> 02:00:09.500
Therefore, a return on investment measure.

02:00:09.500 --> 02:00:11.580
So an ROI measure.

02:00:11.580 --> 02:00:14.460
So it's measuring how many dollars of
profit

02:00:15.020 --> 02:00:19.900
a company generates for every dollar that
is invested in assets.

02:00:21.500 --> 02:00:29.180
So in the context of analyzing a potential
turnaround story, the ROA ratio is likely

02:00:29.180 --> 02:00:32.300
to already be low versus the sector.

02:00:33.180 --> 02:00:43.100
And if a stock has fallen materially
already, and we see that the ROA ratio has
already deteriorated

02:00:43.100 --> 02:00:51.260
rapidly and is now low and stable, then in
combination with other ratios, we may
decide

02:00:51.260 --> 02:00:58.380
that this could be the signs of the
beginning of a turnaround where we're in
phase four or phase five.

02:00:58.380 --> 02:01:07.900
And yes, even better if we see a visible
management operating plan and some noises
about restructuring.

02:01:07.900 --> 02:01:18.380
But like the sales to assets ratio on the
flip side, a low ROA with no restructuring
activity

02:01:18.380 --> 02:01:28.380
and no visible management operating plan,
this may cause us to decide to avoid it as
a turnaround long idea.

02:01:28.380 --> 02:01:37.900
In terms of drawbacks, we do have the same
issue with the ROA ratio as with the sales
to assets ratio.

02:01:38.460 --> 02:01:43.500
That being it doesn't take into account
recent asset investments.

02:01:44.060 --> 02:01:49.820
So if the company invests in a new asset,
makes a big investment in that asset,

02:01:50.380 --> 02:01:58.060
the ratio could look low, but future
earnings estimates could now be going
higher.

02:01:58.060 --> 02:02:02.620
It has to be looked at in the context of
the management operating plan.

02:02:03.420 --> 02:02:07.500
And a restructuring plan can change
everything very quickly.

02:02:09.420 --> 02:02:16.940
Our next balance sheet P&L combo is return
on equity, which can be calculated in two
ways.

02:02:17.900 --> 02:02:25.660
Firstly, net income over shareholders
equity or net income over average
shareholders equity,

02:02:25.660 --> 02:02:32.700
where the average shareholders equity is
calculated as shareholders equity at the
start of the year,

02:02:32.700 --> 02:02:37.500
plus shareholders equity at the end of the
year, divided by two.

02:02:39.180 --> 02:02:48.780
Now a return on equity percentage, it
measures a company's ability to turn
equity investments into a profit.

02:02:49.500 --> 02:02:57.420
And this differs from the ROA, the return
on assets ratio percentage, because that
includes debt.

02:02:58.300 --> 02:03:06.700
However, it is similar in that it's an ROI
measure of profitability and efficiency of
how

02:03:06.700 --> 02:03:12.860
dollars are utilized by the company. Now,
in the context of a turnaround,

02:03:13.580 --> 02:03:17.660
we know that earnings have already been
falling for a while.

02:03:18.460 --> 02:03:24.780
And with some of our earnings growth
profiles, they are forecast to potentially
fall further

02:03:24.780 --> 02:03:27.980
and that we could be in a value trap
scenario.

02:03:29.340 --> 02:03:35.580
So again, to get better context, we have
to look at this metric through time

02:03:36.220 --> 02:03:43.420
and compare it to sector peers to
determine if there's been deterioration or
any improvement.

02:03:43.980 --> 02:03:52.380
And we need to figure out where we are in
the bigger picture by using other
financial statement, quantitative metrics.

02:03:52.380 --> 02:04:00.940
And we need to be figuring out what likely
phase we are in when the company has gone
X growth.

02:04:00.940 --> 02:04:04.860
So are we in phase one, two, three, four
or five?

02:04:06.620 --> 02:04:14.140
So remember, for value traps, for
potential shorts, we don't like phase four
and phase five,

02:04:14.700 --> 02:04:18.140
even if the management operating plan
looks suspect.

02:04:18.780 --> 02:04:25.500
It can be low market cap and there may be
value buyers around and they're probably
not lurking too far away.

02:04:25.500 --> 02:04:35.260
For turnarounds, for longs, we like phase
four and phase five, but we need to see a
convincing management operating plan.

02:04:35.260 --> 02:04:42.540
And we need to time the trade very well
because we are operating in the 20 to 60
day time horizon.

02:04:43.660 --> 02:04:48.060
Now, just as a side note, when we're
comparing ROE versus ROA,

02:04:49.580 --> 02:05:01.900
if ROE is materially higher than ROA, it's
because the denominator in ROA compared to
ROE is much bigger.

02:05:01.900 --> 02:05:05.900
And this will be because the company has a
lot of debt.

02:05:07.340 --> 02:05:13.740
So it's actually a good step to compare
the ROA and the ROE versus each other

02:05:14.300 --> 02:05:22.700
because they're both efficiency ratios for
net income, one over assets and one over
shareholders equity.

02:05:22.700 --> 02:05:31.500
So how efficient the company is in turning
assets into profit and turning equity into
profit.

02:05:32.540 --> 02:05:37.980
And looking at the difference between the
two to get a better understanding of the
stock.

02:05:38.780 --> 02:05:46.460
And in the context of a turnaround story
or even identifying something that's early
stage X growth,

02:05:47.660 --> 02:05:56.140
looking at how efficient a company is and
how efficient it's being run helps us in
assessing

02:05:56.140 --> 02:06:03.340
the possibility of whether a company will
continue its X growth slide

02:06:04.300 --> 02:06:11.500
or the probability that the company can
turn around the fortunes of earnings.

02:06:14.060 --> 02:06:19.180
Now, the last metric that we're going to
look at is a cash flow metric.

02:06:19.180 --> 02:06:22.940
And we're going to look briefly at free
cash flow yield.

02:06:23.580 --> 02:06:34.780
But a derivative of this that we've worked
out is actually more important, which is
the price over free cash flow per share
metric.

02:06:36.620 --> 02:06:43.100
So firstly, if we look at free cash flow
yield and look at the calculation of that,

02:06:43.660 --> 02:06:50.860
it is the last annual free cash flow per
share over the current market stock price.

02:06:50.860 --> 02:06:56.220
So this is the backwards looking metric
for free cash flow yield.

02:06:56.860 --> 02:07:01.820
There is also another calculation, which
is a forward looking metric,

02:07:01.820 --> 02:07:08.700
which is the annualized free cash flow per
share over the current market stock price.

02:07:09.260 --> 02:07:17.900
But we can work this out the other way
around and we can get the multiple that
the market is willing to pay

02:07:17.900 --> 02:07:22.700
for the free cash flows of the company on
a per share basis.

02:07:23.740 --> 02:07:27.580
And we can get this as a forward looking
measure.

02:07:27.580 --> 02:07:35.100
So instead of doing annualized free cash
flow per share over current market stock
price,

02:07:35.100 --> 02:07:36.220
we flip this around.

02:07:36.780 --> 02:07:43.340
So we do current market stock price
divided by annualized free cash flow per
share.

02:07:43.900 --> 02:07:49.980
And what that gives us is the price over
free cash flow per share metric,

02:07:50.940 --> 02:07:58.300
which as traders and investors, we treat
in a similar way to price earnings ratio,

02:07:58.300 --> 02:08:01.020
where we have the stock price over EPS.

02:08:01.660 --> 02:08:07.340
In this instance, we have the stock price
over free cash flow per share.

02:08:08.780 --> 02:08:11.580
And similar to price earnings ratio,

02:08:12.380 --> 02:08:21.500
academia in finance will teach you that
